ML_PAGE("l2-notes", "<header class=\"page-header theme-l2\"><span class=\"eyebrow\">CHAPTER 2 · COMPLETE REFERENCE</span><h2>Full Intuitive Explanation</h2><p>The complete Chapter 2 narrative, preserved in full. Use the guided lessons for a first pass; open this when a concept still isn't clicking.</p></header><div class=\"action-row\"><button class=\"primary-btn bg-l2\" data-action=\"print\">Print this page</button><button class=\"secondary-btn\" data-nav=\"l2-subjective\">Go to subjective prep</button><button class=\"secondary-btn\" data-nav=\"l2-prep\">Go to MCQ prep</button></div><details class=\"topic-index toc-panel theme-l2\" id=\"l2n-toc\"><summary><strong>Reading order</strong><span class=\"toc-count\">59 sections</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><input type=\"search\" class=\"toc-search\" placeholder=\"Filter sections...\" aria-label=\"Filter sections\"><ul class=\"toc-list\"><li data-full=\"Market classifications: two different questions\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l2n-s1\" title=\"Market classifications: two different questions\">Market classifications</button></li><li data-full=\"Money market vs capital market: think time horizon\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l2n-s2\" title=\"Money market vs capital market: think time horizon\">Money market vs capital market</button></li><li data-full=\"Primary vs secondary market: follow the money\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l2n-s3\" title=\"Primary vs secondary market: follow the money\">Primary vs secondary market</button></li><li data-full=\"Why secondary markets matter if the company doesn&#x27;t receive the money\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l2n-s4\" title=\"Why secondary markets matter if the company doesn&#x27;t receive the money\">Why secondary markets matter</button></li><li data-full=\"The IPO: how a private company enters the public market\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l2n-s5\" title=\"The IPO: how a private company enters the public market\">The IPO</button></li><li data-full=\"IPOs force five hard questions\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l2n-s6\" title=\"IPOs force five hard questions\">IPOs force five hard questions</button></li><li data-full=\"The IPO process intuitively\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l2n-s7\" title=\"The IPO process intuitively\">The IPO process intuitively</button></li><li data-full=\"Prospectus: reducing information asymmetry\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l2n-s8\" title=\"Prospectus: reducing information asymmetry\">Prospectus</button></li><li data-full=\"Red herring, quiet period and roadshow\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l2n-s9\" title=\"Red herring, quiet period and roadshow\">Red herring, quiet period and roadshow</button></li><li data-full=\"Why an underwriting syndicate exists\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l2n-s10\" title=\"Why an underwriting syndicate exists\">Why an underwriting syndicate exists</button></li><li data-full=\"Why does the investment bank get paid?\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l2n-s11\" title=\"Why does the investment bank get paid?\">Why does the investment bank get paid</button></li><li data-full=\"Broker market vs dealer market\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l2n-s12\" title=\"Broker market vs dealer market\">Broker market vs dealer market</button></li><li data-full=\"Broker: &quot;I&#x27;ll find the other side&quot;\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l2n-s13\" title=\"Broker: &quot;I&#x27;ll find the other side&quot;\">Broker: I'll find the other side</button></li><li data-full=\"Dealer: &quot;I&#x27;ll be the other side&quot;\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l2n-s14\" title=\"Dealer: &quot;I&#x27;ll be the other side&quot;\">Dealer: I'll be the other side</button></li><li data-full=\"Bid and ask: always think from the dealer&#x27;s perspective\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l2n-s15\" title=\"Bid and ask: always think from the dealer&#x27;s perspective\">Bid and ask</button></li><li data-full=\"A useful exam trick for bid and ask\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l2n-s16\" title=\"A useful exam trick for bid and ask\">A useful exam trick for bid and ask</button></li><li data-full=\"Why dealer markets can exist without a physical trading floor\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l2n-s17\" title=\"Why dealer markets can exist without a physical trading floor\">Markets without a trading floor</button></li><li data-full=\"Alternative trading systems\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l2n-s18\" title=\"Alternative trading systems\">Alternative trading systems</button></li><li data-full=\"Bull and bear markets\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l2n-s19\" title=\"Bull and bear markets\">Bull and bear markets</button></li><li data-full=\"Globalization and diversification\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l2n-s20\" title=\"Globalization and diversification\">Globalization and diversification</button></li><li data-full=\"International investing creates new risks\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l2n-s21\" title=\"International investing creates new risks\">International investing creates new risks</button></li><li data-full=\"Regulation: don&#x27;t memorize legislation without understanding the problem\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l2n-s22\" title=\"Regulation: don&#x27;t memorize legislation without understanding the problem\">Regulation</button></li><li data-full=\"Insider trading: why it matters economically\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l2n-s23\" title=\"Insider trading: why it matters economically\">Insider trading</button></li><li data-full=\"Trading hours\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l2n-s24\" title=\"Trading hours\">Trading hours</button></li><li data-full=\"Now the most technical part: securities transactions\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l2n-s25\" title=\"Now the most technical part: securities transactions\">Securities transactions: three positions</button></li><li data-full=\"Long purchase\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l2n-s26\" title=\"Long purchase\">Long purchase</button></li><li data-full=\"Margin trading: you are investing with borrowed money\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l2n-s27\" title=\"Margin trading: you are investing with borrowed money\">Margin trading</button></li><li data-full=\"Why leverage magnifies returns\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l2n-s28\" title=\"Why leverage magnifies returns\">Why leverage magnifies returns</button></li><li data-full=\"And leverage magnifies losses\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l2n-s29\" title=\"And leverage magnifies losses\">And leverage magnifies losses</button></li><li data-full=\"Margin is NOT the amount borrowed\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l2n-s30\" title=\"Margin is NOT the amount borrowed\">Margin is NOT the amount borrowed</button></li><li data-full=\"Example of the margin formula\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l2n-s31\" title=\"Example of the margin formula\">Example of the margin formula</button></li><li data-full=\"Why margin changes even if the loan doesn&#x27;t\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l2n-s32\" title=\"Why margin changes even if the loan doesn&#x27;t\">Why margin changes even if the loan doesn't</button></li><li data-full=\"The most intuitive way to understand a margin call\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l2n-s33\" title=\"The most intuitive way to understand a margin call\">Understanding a margin call</button></li><li data-full=\"Deriving the margin-call price\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l2n-s34\" title=\"Deriving the margin-call price\">Deriving the margin-call price</button></li><li data-full=\"Margin-call example\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l2n-s35\" title=\"Margin-call example\">Margin-call example</button></li><li data-full=\"Return on invested capital under margin\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l2n-s36\" title=\"Return on invested capital under margin\">Return on invested capital under margin</button></li><li data-full=\"Why borrowing costs matter\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l2n-s37\" title=\"Why borrowing costs matter\">Why borrowing costs matter</button></li><li data-full=\"Short selling: reverse the usual order\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l2n-s38\" title=\"Short selling: reverse the usual order\">Short selling</button></li><li data-full=\"Short-sale example\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l2n-s39\" title=\"Short-sale example\">Short-sale example</button></li><li data-full=\"Long vs short: the directional mirror\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l2n-s40\" title=\"Long vs short: the directional mirror\">Long vs short</button></li><li data-full=\"Why short-selling losses can be theoretically unlimited\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l2n-s41\" title=\"Why short-selling losses can be theoretically unlimited\">Why short losses are unlimited</button></li><li data-full=\"Why a stock rising hurts a short seller twice psychologically\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l2n-s42\" title=\"Why a stock rising hurts a short seller twice psychologically\">A rising price and the short seller</button></li><li data-full=\"Margin also matters in short sales\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l2n-s43\" title=\"Margin also matters in short sales\">Margin also matters in short sales</button></li><li data-full=\"Long, margin and short positions compared\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l2n-s44\" title=\"Long, margin and short positions compared\">Long, margin and short positions compared</button></li><li data-full=\"The deeper principle behind margin and shorting\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l2n-s45\" title=\"The deeper principle behind margin and shorting\">The deeper principle behind margin and shorting</button></li><li data-full=\"Evaluate this statement: &quot;Margin increases return&quot;\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l2n-s46\" title=\"Evaluate this statement: &quot;Margin increases return&quot;\">Evaluate: margin increases return</button></li><li data-full=\"Evaluate: &quot;The secondary market is less important because firms don&#x27;t raise money there&quot;\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l2n-s47\" title=\"Evaluate: &quot;The secondary market is less important because firms don&#x27;t raise money there&quot;\">Evaluate: secondary markets are less important</button></li><li data-full=\"Evaluate: &quot;A dealer is basically another name for a broker&quot;\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l2n-s48\" title=\"Evaluate: &quot;A dealer is basically another name for a broker&quot;\">Evaluate: a dealer is just a broker</button></li><li data-full=\"Evaluate: &quot;International diversification automatically lowers risk&quot;\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l2n-s49\" title=\"Evaluate: &quot;International diversification automatically lowers risk&quot;\">Evaluate: going global always lowers risk</button></li><li data-full=\"Evaluate: &quot;If a stock is liquid, it is safe&quot;\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l2n-s50\" title=\"Evaluate: &quot;If a stock is liquid, it is safe&quot;\">Evaluate: liquid means safe</button></li><li data-full=\"A full case to integrate the chapter\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l2n-s51\" title=\"A full case to integrate the chapter\">A full case to integrate the chapter</button></li><li data-full=\"The six things I would master hardest for an exam\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l2n-s52\" title=\"The six things I would master hardest for an exam\">Six things to master</button></li><li data-full=\"Bloom level: Remember\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l2n-s53\" title=\"Bloom level: Remember\">Bloom level: Remember</button></li><li data-full=\"Bloom level: Understand\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l2n-s54\" title=\"Bloom level: Understand\">Bloom level: Understand</button></li><li data-full=\"Bloom level: Apply\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l2n-s55\" title=\"Bloom level: Apply\">Bloom level: Apply</button></li><li data-full=\"Bloom level: Analyse\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l2n-s56\" title=\"Bloom level: Analyse\">Bloom level: Analyse</button></li><li data-full=\"Bloom level: Evaluate\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l2n-s57\" title=\"Bloom level: Evaluate\">Bloom level: Evaluate</button></li><li data-full=\"Bloom level: Create\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l2n-s58\" title=\"Bloom level: Create\">Bloom level: Create</button></li><li data-full=\"The chapter compressed into one mental model\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l2n-s59\" title=\"The chapter compressed into one mental model\">The chapter in one mental model</button></li></ul></details><details class=\"full-notes theme-l2\" open><summary><div><strong>Chapter 2 - complete intuitive explanation</strong><small>Every section of the notes, with worked numbers, maths and interactive visuals.</small></div><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"markdown-body\"><div class=\"notes-toolbar\"><button type=\"button\" data-sections=\"open\">Expand all</button><button type=\"button\" data-sections=\"close\">Collapse all</button><span>59 sections · click a heading to open it</span></div><p>Chapter 2 is considerably more technical than Chapter 1 because it moves from <strong>\"what should I invest in?\"</strong> to <strong>\"how does the machinery of the securities market actually work?\"</strong></p>\n<p>If Chapter 1 was about choosing investments, Chapter 2 is about understanding the infrastructure that allows investments to be <strong>created, distributed, traded, priced, regulated, borrowed, leveraged and shorted</strong>.</p>\n<p>The chapter formally expects you to understand market types, IPOs, broker/dealer markets, international markets, regulation, and long/margin/short transactions.</p>\n<p>The architecture I would keep in your head is:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow boxed\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Security created → Security sold → Security traded → Price discovered → Transaction strategy\"><span>Security created</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Security sold</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Security traded</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Price discovered</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Transaction strategy</span></div>\n<p>And surrounding all of it:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{Regulation}}$$</div>\n<hr />\n<h2>Chapter 2 - Securities Markets and Transactions</h2>\n<h4>The single most important idea</h4>\n<p>A financial market is not just \"a place where stocks are bought.\"</p>\n<p>It performs several different economic functions.</p>\n<p>A company may need to <strong>raise money</strong>.</p>\n<p>An investor may need to <strong>sell an existing investment</strong>.</p>\n<p>A market maker may provide <strong>liquidity</strong>.</p>\n<p>A regulator may enforce <strong>information disclosure</strong>.</p>\n<p>An investor may borrow money to increase exposure.</p>\n<p>Another investor may borrow shares because they expect the price to fall.</p>\n<p>Those are very different activities.</p>\n<p>Chapter 2 teaches you not to confuse them.</p>\n<hr /><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l2n-s1\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Market classifications: two different questions</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><div class=\"notes-visual\"><span>Visual</span><figure class=\"vis-figure vis-sketch\" data-diagram=\"l2_market_grid\"><div class=\"sketch-slot\"></div><figcaption>Primary/secondary and money/capital are independent dimensions: every security sits in one cell of each.</figcaption></figure></div><p>The chapter introduces four major market labels:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Question</th>\n<th>Classification</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>How long until the security matures?</td>\n<td>Money market vs capital market</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Has the security already been issued?</td>\n<td>Primary vs secondary market</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>The slides define money markets as markets for short-term securities and capital markets as markets for longer-term securities such as stocks and bonds. Primary markets deal with newly issued securities, while secondary markets deal with securities after issuance.</p>\n<p>The crucial thing is:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>These are <strong>two separate dimensions</strong>, not four mutually exclusive boxes.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>For example, a newly issued 10-year corporate bond is simultaneously:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{Capital market} + \\text{Primary market}}$$</div>\n<p>Six months later, when Investor A sells that same bond to Investor B:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{Capital market} + \\text{Secondary market}}$$</div>\n<p>Similarly, a newly issued short-term Treasury instrument could be:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{Money market} + \\text{Primary market}}$$</div>\n<p>This is a classic point lecturers can test indirectly.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l2n-s2\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Money market vs capital market: think time horizon</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Forget the word \"money.\" The distinction is basically about maturity.</p>\n<h4>Money market</h4>\n<p>Short-term securities.</p>\n<p>Conceptually:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{temporary financing / liquidity management}$$</div>\n<h4>Capital market</h4>\n<p>Longer-term financing.</p>\n<p>Conceptually:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{long-term capital formation}$$</div>\n<p>Stocks are naturally capital-market instruments because common stock does not mature in one year.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l2n-s3\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Primary vs secondary market: follow the money</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>This distinction is more important.</p>\n<p>Suppose a company issues new shares for RM10 each.</p>\n<p>You subscribe to the new issue.</p>\n<p>Your RM10 goes ultimately toward financing the company.</p>\n<p>That is:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{Primary market}}$$</div>\n<p>Now six months later you sell your share to your friend for RM14.</p>\n<p>The company does <strong>not</strong> receive that RM14.</p>\n<p>You do.</p>\n<p>That is:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{Secondary market}}$$</div>\n<p>So the best test is:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Is the issuer raising new capital, or are existing investors trading among themselves?</strong></p>\n</blockquote>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l2n-s4\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Why secondary markets matter if the company doesn't receive the money</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>This is where surface-level explanations tend to fall apart.</p>\n<p>The slides identify two key functions of secondary markets:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{Liquidity}}$$</div>\n<p>and</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{Continuous pricing}}$$</div>\n<p>Imagine buying shares in a company knowing:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>\"Once you buy them, you can never sell them.\"</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Obviously you would be much less willing to invest.</p>\n<p>The secondary market gives you an exit mechanism.</p>\n<p>That makes securities more attractive in the first place.</p>\n<p>It also produces a constantly changing market price.</p>\n<p>So:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Trading activity}\n\\rightarrow\n\\text{Price discovery}$$</div>\n<p>and:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Ability to resell}\n\\rightarrow\n\\text{Liquidity}$$</div>\n<p>This is why the secondary market remains economically important even though ordinary resale transactions don't directly raise fresh capital for the company.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l2n-s5\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">The IPO: how a private company enters the public market</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>An IPO is the first public sale of a company's stock. The chapter classifies it as a primary-market transaction.</p>\n<p>Don't think:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>IPO = company's stock starts trading.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Think:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\n\\text{Company ownership claims are being created/distributed to public investors}\n}$$</div>\n<p>The company is essentially saying:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>\"We will sell part of the ownership of this company to outside investors in exchange for capital.\"</p>\n</blockquote>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l2n-s6\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">IPOs force five hard questions</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Going public sounds simple:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Decide how many shares → sell them.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Reality is harder. The company must answer five hard questions, and each one is answered by a step of the IPO process:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>#</th>\n<th>The hard question</th>\n<th>Who or what answers it</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1</td>\n<td><strong>What is the company worth?</strong></td>\n<td>The investment bank's valuation of the company</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2</td>\n<td><strong>What price should the shares be sold at?</strong></td>\n<td>The road show: investor interest helps set the offering price</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3</td>\n<td><strong>Who will buy them?</strong></td>\n<td>The underwriting syndicate and the selling group distribute the shares</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4</td>\n<td><strong>What information must investors receive?</strong></td>\n<td>The prospectus (and the red herring before it)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>5</td>\n<td><strong>What if there isn't enough demand?</strong></td>\n<td>Underwriting: the bank buys the issue and bears the resale risk</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>That is why investment banks become important.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l2n-s7\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">The IPO process intuitively</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><div class=\"notes-visual\"><span>Visual</span><figure class=\"vis-figure vis-sketch\" data-diagram=\"l2_ipo_chain\"><div class=\"sketch-slot\"></div><figcaption>Underwriters carry the resale risk and are paid through the spread between their buying and offering price.</figcaption></figure></div><div class=\"notes-visual\"><span>Visual</span><figure class=\"vis-figure vis-sketch\" data-diagram=\"l2_underwriting\"><div class=\"sketch-slot\"></div><figcaption>The point of underwriting: the company swaps an uncertain amount of cash for a certain one, and the bank carries the risk of weak demand.</figcaption></figure></div><div class=\"notes-visual\"><span>Visual</span><figure class=\"vis-figure vis-widget\" data-vis=\"l2_underwrite\"><div class=\"vis-title\">Who carries the risk in underwriting</div><div class=\"vis-root\"></div><figcaption>Interactive · Change what the public will pay for the shares and see who ends up carrying the shortfall.</figcaption></figure></div><p>The chapter introduces underwriting, prospectuses, red herrings, quiet periods and roadshows.</p>\n<p>Rather than memorize five definitions, think of the process as:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow boxed\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Prepare → Disclose → Market → Price → Sell\"><span>Prepare</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Disclose</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Market</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Price</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Sell</span></div>\n<h4>Underwriting</h4>\n<p>The investment banker helps promote and facilitate the sale of the shares.</p>\n<p>More importantly, the chapter describes underwriting as the investment bank purchasing the issue at an agreed price and bearing the risk of reselling it to public investors.</p>\n<p>Economically, this transfers distribution risk.</p>\n<p>The company doesn't want to discover at the last minute:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>\"Apparently nobody wants our RM1 billion issue.\"</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>The underwriter takes on part of that risk.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l2n-s8\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Prospectus: reducing information asymmetry</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><div class=\"notes-visual\"><span>Visual</span><figure class=\"vis-figure vis-sketch\" data-diagram=\"l2_prospectus\"><div class=\"sketch-slot\"></div><figcaption>The point of a prospectus: move the facts insiders know to the outsiders who must decide what the shares are worth.</figcaption></figure></div><p>Imagine a company asking you:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>\"Give me RM10,000 and become a shareholder.\"</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Naturally you would ask:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>What does the company do?</p>\n<p>How profitable is it?</p>\n<p>What are its risks?</p>\n<p>What exactly am I buying?</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>This is why a prospectus matters.</p>\n<p>The chapter describes it as the registration statement describing the issue and issuer.</p>\n<p>Conceptually:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{Prospectus = standardized information for investors}}$$</div>\n<p>It exists partly because insiders know much more about the company than outside investors.</p>\n<p>That problem is called <strong>information asymmetry</strong>.</p>\n<p>The prospectus cannot eliminate it completely, but disclosure reduces it.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l2n-s9\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Red herring, quiet period and roadshow</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><div class=\"notes-visual\"><span>Visual</span><figure class=\"vis-figure vis-sketch\" data-diagram=\"l2_red_herring\"><div class=\"sketch-slot\"></div><figcaption>The point of the red herring and the quiet period: before the sale, investors judge the issue from one reviewed document, not from hype.</figcaption></figure></div><div class=\"notes-visual\"><span>Visual</span><figure class=\"vis-figure vis-sketch\" data-diagram=\"l2_roadshow\"><div class=\"sketch-slot\"></div><figcaption>The point of the road show: collect orders from big investors and use them to set the offering price.</figcaption></figure></div><p>A <strong>red herring</strong> is the preliminary prospectus used during the waiting period.</p>\n<p>The <strong>quiet period</strong> restricts what the company says after filing.</p>\n<p>The <strong>roadshow</strong> involves presentations to prospective investors.</p>\n<p>The roadshow is especially intuitive.</p>\n<p>Investment bankers aren't just saying:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>\"Here's a company. Please buy.\"</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>They are testing demand.</p>\n<p>Potential institutional investors effectively reveal information such as:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>\"At this valuation we're interested.\"</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>or:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>\"At that price we're not.\"</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>So IPO marketing also helps discover an appropriate offering price.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l2n-s10\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Why an underwriting syndicate exists</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Suppose the IPO is enormous.</p>\n<p>One investment bank taking the entire risk would create substantial exposure.</p>\n<p>So the lead investment banker can form an <strong>underwriting syndicate</strong> to share the financial risk. A broader <strong>selling group</strong> can then help distribute shares.</p>\n<p>The diagram on slide 10 shows this structure visually:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">        Issuing company\n              │  sells the whole issue at an agreed price\n              ▼\n  Lead investment banker + underwriting syndicate   (share the underwriting risk)\n              │\n              ▼\n          Selling group                              (widens distribution)\n              │\n              ▼\n           Investors\n</code></pre>\n<p>The key principle is:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{large issue} \\Rightarrow \\text{risk sharing + wider distribution}}$$</div>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l2n-s11\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Why does the investment bank get paid?</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>The chapter says compensation usually comes through a discount on the securities' sale price.</p>\n<p>Imagine:</p>\n<p>Investment bank purchases shares from issuer for:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\$19$$</div>\n<p>and distributes them to investors at:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\$20$$</div>\n<p>The difference helps compensate it for:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{distribution work}\n+\n\\text{underwriting risk}\n+\n\\text{marketing}$$</div>\n<p>The precise institutional mechanics can vary, but that's the economic logic the chapter wants you to understand.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l2n-s12\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Broker market vs dealer market</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><div class=\"notes-visual\"><span>Visual</span><figure class=\"vis-figure vis-sketch\" data-diagram=\"l2_broker_dealer\"><div class=\"sketch-slot\"></div><figcaption>Read a quote from the dealer&#x27;s side: the dealer buys at the bid and sells at the ask.</figcaption></figure></div><p>This distinction is extremely testable.</p>\n<p>The chapter says that in a broker market, a buyer and seller are brought together and transact with each other.</p>\n<p>In a dealer market, a <strong>market maker</strong> stands between them.</p>\n<p>The cleanest way to remember it is:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{Broker = agent}}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{Dealer = principal}}$$</div>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l2n-s13\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Broker: \"I'll find the other side\"</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Suppose you want to sell 100 shares.</p>\n<p>The broker helps match you with somebody wanting to buy them.</p>\n<p>Conceptually:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Seller}\n\\rightarrow\n\\boxed{\\text{Broker}}\n\\leftarrow\n\\text{Buyer}$$</div>\n<p>The broker facilitates the transaction.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l2n-s14\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Dealer: \"I'll be the other side\"</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>A dealer behaves differently.</p>\n<p>Suppose no buyer appears immediately.</p>\n<p>The dealer may buy from you.</p>\n<p>Then later sell those shares to another investor.</p>\n<p>So:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Seller}\n\\rightarrow\n\\boxed{\\text{Dealer}}\n\\rightarrow\n\\text{Buyer}$$</div>\n<p>The dealer is actually transacting.</p>\n<p>Therefore the dealer may carry inventory risk.</p>\n<p>That is why the term <strong>market maker</strong> matters.</p>\n<p>The dealer helps ensure there is someone available to trade with.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l2n-s15\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Bid and ask: always think from the dealer's perspective</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>The chapter defines:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Bid Price}\n=\n\\text{highest price dealer offers to BUY}$$</div>\n<p>and</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Ask Price}\n=\n\\text{lowest price dealer offers to SELL}$$</div>\n<p>Suppose the quote is:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\$99.50/\\$100.00$$</div>\n<p>Then:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Bid}=99.50$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Ask}=100.00$$</div>\n<p>If <strong>you sell</strong>, the dealer buys from you.</p>\n<p>You receive:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\$99.50$$</div>\n<p>If <strong>you buy</strong>, the dealer sells to you.</p>\n<p>You pay:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\$100.00$$</div>\n<p>Hence:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{Bid}&lt;\\text{Ask}}$$</div>\n<p>The difference is the <strong>bid-ask spread</strong>:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$100.00-99.50=0.50$$</div>\n<p>Intuitively, part of that spread compensates the dealer for providing liquidity and carrying trading/inventory risk.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l2n-s16\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">A useful exam trick for bid and ask</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Ask yourself:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>What does the dealer want to do?</strong></p>\n</blockquote>\n<p><strong>Bid = dealer bids to BUY.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Ask = dealer asks a price to SELL.</strong></p>\n<p>This prevents the common mistake of thinking:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>\"Ask means what the investor asks to buy at.\"</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>No.</p>\n<p>View the quote from the dealer's side.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l2n-s17\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Why dealer markets can exist without a physical trading floor</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>The chapter describes dealer markets as networks of market makers connected electronically rather than requiring a centralized trading floor.</p>\n<p>That's important because:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{A market is a mechanism, not necessarily a place}}$$</div>\n<p>Once orders, quotes and counterparties can interact electronically, the market does not need everybody shouting across one room.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l2n-s18\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Alternative trading systems</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><div class=\"notes-visual\"><span>Visual</span><figure class=\"vis-figure vis-sketch\" data-diagram=\"l2_trading_landscape\"><div class=\"sketch-slot\"></div><figcaption>The four trading venues differ in who stands between buyer and seller. Big institutions moved down the ladder to cut costs.</figcaption></figure></div><p>The chapter introduces the <strong>third market</strong> and <strong>fourth market</strong>.</p>\n<p>The third market involves institutional investors trading through market makers that are not exchange members.</p>\n<p>The fourth market goes further: institutional investors transact directly with each other, often through electronic communication networks, bypassing market makers.</p>\n<p>Think of the progression:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Traditional exchange → non-exchange dealer → direct institution-to-institution trading\"><span>Traditional exchange</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>non-exchange dealer</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>direct institution-to-institution trading</span></div>\n<p>Why?</p>\n<p>Cost.</p>\n<p>Imagine a pension fund trading RM500 million worth of securities.</p>\n<p>A tiny transaction cost matters enormously.</p>\n<p>Even:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$0.10\\%$$</div>\n<p>on RM500 million equals:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{RM}500{,}000$$</div>\n<p>So institutions have strong incentives to reduce unnecessary intermediation.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l2n-s19\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Bull and bear markets</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>The slides associate bull markets with rising prices, optimism, economic growth/recovery and stimulus, while bear markets are associated with falling prices, pessimism and economic slowdown.</p>\n<p>Don't answer an exam question with:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Bull = prices up.</p>\n<p>Bear = prices down.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>That's definition-level thinking.</p>\n<p>A better causal structure is:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Economic expectations → Expected corporate performance → Investor expectations → Demand for securities → Market prices\"><span>Economic expectations</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Expected corporate performance</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Investor expectations</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Demand for securities</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Market prices</span></div>\n<p>But be careful with causality.</p>\n<p>Optimism can support higher prices, while rising prices can themselves increase optimism.</p>\n<p>Markets are feedback systems.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l2n-s20\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Globalization and diversification</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>The chapter argues that international securities can improve diversification because investors gain access to more industries, different currencies and rapidly expanding economies. It also notes that foreign markets do not necessarily move together with the U.S. market.</p>\n<p>The deeper point is <strong>correlation</strong>.</p>\n<p>Suppose your entire portfolio is Malaysian equities.</p>\n<p>If Malaysia experiences an economic shock, much of your portfolio could decline simultaneously.</p>\n<p>Now suppose you own assets across multiple economies.</p>\n<p>If those markets do not move perfectly together:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{poor performance somewhere may be partially offset elsewhere}}$$</div>\n<p>That's where diversification actually comes from.</p>\n<p>Not simply:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>\"Own lots of things.\"</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>But:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{Own exposures whose risks are not perfectly aligned}}$$</div>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l2n-s21\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">International investing creates new risks</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>The chapter identifies government-policy risk and currency-exchange-rate risk, alongside the usual investment risks.</p>\n<p>Currency risk deserves special attention.</p>\n<p>Suppose you're a Malaysian investor.</p>\n<p>You invest:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{RM}10{,}000$$</div>\n<p>in a U.S. stock.</p>\n<p>Suppose the stock rises:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$+10\\%$$</div>\n<p>in USD.</p>\n<p>You might think:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>\"I earned 10%.\"</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Not necessarily.</p>\n<p>If the USD weakens against the ringgit during the same period, part-or all-of that return can disappear when converted back to RM.</p>\n<p>Conceptually:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$(1+R_{\\text{home currency}})\n=\n(1+R_{\\text{foreign asset}})\n(1+R_{\\text{currency}})$$</div>\n<p>Suppose:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$R_{\\text{asset}}=10\\%$$</div>\n<p>and the foreign currency depreciates by:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$12\\%$$</div>\n<p>Then:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$(1.10)(0.88)-1$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$=-3.2\\%$$</div>\n<p>The security made money in its home currency.</p>\n<p>You lost money in yours.</p>\n<p>That is why:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{foreign asset return} \\neq \\text{domestic investor return}}$$</div>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l2n-s22\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Regulation: don't memorize legislation without understanding the problem</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>The chapter presents several U.S. securities laws.</p>\n<p>Instead of memorizing a graveyard of dates, ask:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>What failure is each regulation trying to address?</strong></p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>The Securities Act of 1933 emphasizes disclosure, the 1934 Act established the SEC, later regulation addresses self-regulation, investment companies, investment advisers, insider trading and corporate-accounting abuses.</p>\n<p>The underlying problems are:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{information asymmetry}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{fraud}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{conflicts of interest}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{market manipulation}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{unfair informational advantages}$$</div>\n<p>That conceptual framework matters more than blindly reciting 1933, 1934, 1938, 1940...</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l2n-s23\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Insider trading: why it matters economically</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>The chapter defines insider trading here as using non-public company information to make profitable securities transactions.</p>\n<p>Why is this dangerous to a market?</p>\n<p>Imagine you know:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Company X will announce bankruptcy tomorrow.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Nobody else knows.</p>\n<p>You sell today.</p>\n<p>The other side of the transaction is trading without access to information you possess.</p>\n<p>If investors start believing markets are rigged in favour of privileged insiders:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Trust}\\downarrow$$</div>\n<p>which can damage willingness to participate.</p>\n<p>So market regulation isn't merely punishment.</p>\n<p>It helps preserve confidence in the market mechanism.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l2n-s24\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Trading hours</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>The chapter gives U.S. regular trading hours and historical extended-hours sessions.</p>\n<p>For your course, use the slide information if the lecturer tests it.</p>\n<p>But notice the age of the source: the chapter is copyrighted 2014, and its IPO table only runs through 2011.</p>\n<p>So treat exact exchange statistics, specific market structures and historical trading-session details as <strong>course material</strong>, not automatically current market facts.</p>\n<p>The concepts remain useful even when market infrastructure evolves.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l2n-s25\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Now the most technical part: securities transactions</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>There are three major positions:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Transaction</th>\n<th>Core view</th>\n<th>How profit arises</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Long purchase</td>\n<td>Price will rise</td>\n<td>Buy low → sell high</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Margin purchase</td>\n<td>Price will rise, with leverage</td>\n<td>Same, but borrowed money magnifies result</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Short sale</td>\n<td>Price will fall</td>\n<td>Sell high → buy back low</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>The rest of the chapter becomes much easier if you understand those three economically.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l2n-s26\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Long purchase</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>The chapter defines a long purchase as buying and holding securities with the expectation of making money when prices rise.</p>\n<p>Suppose:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$P_0=\\text{RM}10$$</div>\n<p>and later:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$P_1=\\text{RM}14$$</div>\n<p>Ignoring dividends:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Profit}=14-10=\\text{RM}4$$</div>\n<p>Return:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\frac{4}{10}=40\\%$$</div>\n<p>The directional exposure is:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{P\\uparrow \\Rightarrow \\text{Investor gains}}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{P\\downarrow \\Rightarrow \\text{Investor loses}}$$</div>\n<p>Simple.</p>\n<p>Margin and short selling complicate that payoff.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l2n-s27\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Margin trading: you are investing with borrowed money</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>The chapter defines margin trading as using borrowed funds to purchase securities, with securities serving as collateral.</p>\n<p>Suppose you want RM10,000 of stock.</p>\n<p>Instead of putting up RM10,000 yourself, you contribute:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{RM}5{,}000$$</div>\n<p>and borrow:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{RM}5{,}000$$</div>\n<p>You still control:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{RM}10{,}000$$</div>\n<p>of stock.</p>\n<p>That's leverage.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l2n-s28\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Why leverage magnifies returns</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Suppose the RM10,000 stock position rises by 20%.</p>\n<p>Its new value is:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{RM}12{,}000$$</div>\n<p>Profit:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{RM}2{,}000$$</div>\n<p>If you had funded the full RM10,000:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$R=\\frac{2{,}000}{10{,}000}=20\\%$$</div>\n<p>But with only RM5,000 of your own equity:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$R=\\frac{2{,}000}{5{,}000}=40\\%$$</div>\n<p>ignoring borrowing cost.</p>\n<p>The asset only moved:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$20\\%$$</div>\n<p>Your equity moved:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$40\\%$$</div>\n<p>That is leverage.</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{Smaller equity base} \\Rightarrow \\text{greater percentage effect}}$$</div>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l2n-s29\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">And leverage magnifies losses</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Now suppose that RM10,000 investment falls 20%.</p>\n<p>New value:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{RM}8{,}000$$</div>\n<p>Loss:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{RM}2{,}000$$</div>\n<p>Relative to your RM5,000 equity:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\frac{-2{,}000}{5{,}000}\n=\n-40\\%$$</div>\n<p>Exactly why the chapter lists both:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{magnifies profits}$$</div>\n<p>and</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{magnifies losses}$$</div>\n<p>as consequences of margin trading. It also identifies interest costs and margin calls as disadvantages.</p>\n<p>Leverage does not magically improve the investment.</p>\n<p>It amplifies your exposure.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l2n-s30\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Margin is NOT the amount borrowed</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><div class=\"notes-visual\"><span>Visual</span><figure class=\"vis-figure vis-sketch\" data-diagram=\"l2_margin_sheet\"><div class=\"sketch-slot\"></div><figcaption>The loan does not move, so every fall in value comes straight out of the investor&#x27;s equity.</figcaption></figure></div><p>This is a common mistake.</p>\n<p>The chapter's margin formula is:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\n\\text{Margin}\n=\n\\frac{V-D}{V}\n}$$</div>\n<p>where:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$V=\\text{market value of securities}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$D=\\text{debit balance/loan}$$</div>\n<p>Since:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$V-D=\\text{investor's equity}$$</div>\n<p>you can rewrite it as:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\n\\text{Margin}\n=\n\\frac{\\text{Investor Equity}}\n{\\text{Market Value}}\n}$$</div>\n<p>That interpretation is much easier.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l2n-s31\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Example of the margin formula</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Suppose:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$V=\\text{RM}10{,}000$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$D=\\text{RM}4{,}000$$</div>\n<p>Then your equity is:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$E=10{,}000-4{,}000=6{,}000$$</div>\n<p>Margin:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$m=\\frac{6{,}000}{10{,}000}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{m=60\\%}$$</div>\n<p>This means <strong>60% of the current position value belongs economically to your equity</strong>, while the remainder corresponds to borrowing.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l2n-s32\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Why margin changes even if the loan doesn't</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>This is the clever part.</p>\n<p>Suppose the stock falls from:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{RM}10{,}000$$</div>\n<p>to:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{RM}7{,}000$$</div>\n<p>Your loan remains:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{RM}4{,}000$$</div>\n<p>So equity becomes:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$7{,}000-4{,}000=3{,}000$$</div>\n<p>Margin:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\frac{3{,}000}{7{,}000}\n=\n42.86\\%$$</div>\n<p>Notice:</p>\n<p>Stock fell:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$30\\%$$</div>\n<p>but your equity fell:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\frac{6{,}000-3{,}000}{6{,}000}=50\\%$$</div>\n<p>That's leverage in action.</p>\n<p>The debt did not absorb the market loss.</p>\n<p><strong>Your equity did.</strong></p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l2n-s33\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">The most intuitive way to understand a margin call</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><div class=\"notes-visual\"><span>Visual</span><figure class=\"vis-figure vis-widget\" data-vis=\"l2_margin\"><div class=\"vis-title\">Margin trading</div><div class=\"vis-root\"></div><figcaption>Interactive · Change the margin and the share price; watch equity, return on equity and the margin-call line.</figcaption></figure></div><p>The broker is effectively asking:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>\"Is there still enough of your own money in this position to protect the loan I've given you?\"</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>As the asset price falls:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$V\\downarrow$$</div>\n<p>while:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$D\\approx\\text{fixed}$$</div>\n<p>Therefore:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\frac{V-D}{V}\\downarrow$$</div>\n<p>Eventually the ratio becomes too low.</p>\n<p>Then:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{Margin Call}}$$</div>\n<p>You may need to contribute more funds or otherwise reduce the position.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l2n-s34\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Deriving the margin-call price</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>This isn't explicitly presented as a separate formula on the slides, but we can derive it directly from the chapter's margin equation.</p>\n<p>Suppose the required maintenance margin is:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$m$$</div>\n<p>We know:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$m=\\frac{V-D}{V}$$</div>\n<p>Rearrange:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$mV=V-D$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$D=V(1-m)$$</div>\n<p>Therefore:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\nV_{\\text{critical}}\n=\n\\frac{D}{1-m}\n}$$</div>\n<p>If you own $N$ shares:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$V=NP$$</div>\n<p>so:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\nP_{\\text{margin call}}\n=\n\\frac{D}{N(1-m)}\n}$$</div>\n<p>This is much better than memorizing a margin-call formula because you've derived it from the underlying logic.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l2n-s35\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Margin-call example</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>You buy 100 shares at RM50.</p>\n<p>Total position:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$100(50)=\\text{RM}5{,}000$$</div>\n<p>Assume 50% initial margin.</p>\n<p>Your money:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{RM}2{,}500$$</div>\n<p>Loan:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$D=\\text{RM}2{,}500$$</div>\n<p>Suppose maintenance margin is 30%.</p>\n<p>Then:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$V_{\\text{critical}}\n=\n\\frac{2{,}500}{1-0.30}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$=\n\\text{RM}3{,}571.43$$</div>\n<p>Since there are 100 shares:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$P_{\\text{critical}}\n=\n\\frac{3{,}571.43}{100}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{RM}35.71}$$</div>\n<p>So when the share price falls to roughly RM35.71, your margin reaches 30%.</p>\n<p>This is a Bloom <strong>Analyse/Create</strong> level approach because you can derive the threshold instead of merely identifying that margin calls exist.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l2n-s36\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Return on invested capital under margin</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>The chapter gives the return structure as:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Return}\n=\n\\frac{\n\\text{income}\n-\n\\text{interest}\n+\n\\text{sale value}\n-\n\\text{purchase value}\n}{\n\\text{initial investor equity}\n}$$</div>\n<p>The denominator is extremely important.</p>\n<p>It isn't the entire asset value.</p>\n<p>It is:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{your equity}}$$</div>\n<p>That is why margin can dramatically increase percentage returns.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l2n-s37\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Why borrowing costs matter</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Suppose leverage turns a 20% asset gain into a 40% equity gain.</p>\n<p>Wonderful?</p>\n<p>Not automatically.</p>\n<p>If the margin loan charges interest:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Net return}\n=\n\\text{leveraged gain}\n-\n\\text{financing cost}$$</div>\n<p>The chapter explicitly includes interest paid in the return formula.</p>\n<p>Therefore:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{Leverage increases exposure, not free wealth}}$$</div>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l2n-s38\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Short selling: reverse the usual order</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><div class=\"notes-visual\"><span>Visual</span><figure class=\"vis-figure vis-sketch\" data-diagram=\"l2_short_steps\"><div class=\"sketch-slot\"></div><figcaption>A short sale reverses the order of buying and selling; its gain is capped but its loss is not.</figcaption></figure></div><p>Normally:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Buy}\n\\rightarrow\n\\text{Sell}$$</div>\n<p>Short selling reverses it:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow boxed\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Borrow → Sell → Buy back → Return shares\"><span>Borrow</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Sell</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Buy back</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Return shares</span></div>\n<p>The chapter says that a short seller sells securities they do not own by borrowing them through a broker, hoping to profit when the price declines.</p>\n<p>The slogan is:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{Sell high, buy low}}$$</div>\n<p>rather than:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Buy low, sell high}$$</div>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l2n-s39\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Short-sale example</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Borrow 100 shares trading at:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{RM}50$$</div>\n<p>Sell them:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$100(50)=\\text{RM}5{,}000$$</div>\n<p>Later the price drops to:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{RM}30$$</div>\n<p>Buy back:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$100(30)=\\text{RM}3{,}000$$</div>\n<p>Return the 100 shares.</p>\n<p>Profit before other costs:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$5{,}000-3{,}000$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{RM}2{,}000}$$</div>\n<p>The chapter's mechanics table on slide 36 uses exactly this basic structure.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l2n-s40\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Long vs short: the directional mirror</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>For a long investor:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{P\\uparrow \\Rightarrow \\text{profit}}$$</div>\n<p>For a short seller:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{P\\downarrow \\Rightarrow \\text{profit}}$$</div>\n<p>That sounds symmetrical.</p>\n<p>But the risk structure is <strong>not symmetrical</strong>.</p>\n<p>And this is one of the most important ideas in the chapter.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l2n-s41\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Why short-selling losses can be theoretically unlimited</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><div class=\"notes-visual\"><span>Visual</span><figure class=\"vis-figure vis-widget\" data-vis=\"l2_short\"><div class=\"vis-title\">Long versus short payoff</div><div class=\"vis-root\"></div><figcaption>Interactive · Compare the profit of a long and a short position as the share price moves.</figcaption></figure></div><p>Suppose you buy a stock for:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{RM}50$$</div>\n<p>The worst possible share price is:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{RM}0$$</div>\n<p>So the maximum price loss per share on an unlevered long position is:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{RM}50$$</div>\n<p>But short the stock at RM50.</p>\n<p>What's the highest the price could theoretically rise?</p>\n<p>RM100?</p>\n<p>RM500?</p>\n<p>RM5,000?</p>\n<p>There is no fixed mathematical ceiling.</p>\n<p>Therefore:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{Maximum short-sale gain is limited}}$$</div>\n<p>because the stock cannot fall below zero.</p>\n<p>But:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{Potential short-sale loss is theoretically unlimited}}$$</div>\n<p>because the price can continue rising.</p>\n<p>The chapter explicitly emphasizes this asymmetry.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l2n-s42\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Why a stock rising hurts a short seller twice psychologically</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Suppose you short at:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{RM}50$$</div>\n<p>Price becomes:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{RM}100$$</div>\n<p>You need to buy back at RM100 something you originally sold for RM50.</p>\n<p>Loss:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{RM}50$$</div>\n<p>Then suppose it reaches:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{RM}200$$</div>\n<p>Loss:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{RM}150$$</div>\n<p>The painful part is that the market can continue moving against you while your obligation to return the borrowed shares remains.</p>\n<p>That's why \"I'll just wait until it falls again\" is far more dangerous for a short seller than it sounds.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l2n-s43\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Margin also matters in short sales</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>The chapter's final table shows margin positions for a short sale. Its central lesson is that if the shorted stock rises, the amount required to repurchase it rises while the short seller's account equity shrinks.</p>\n<p>Conceptually:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Short-sale account equity}\n=\n\\text{Funds held by broker}\n-\n\\text{current repurchase cost}$$</div>\n<p>If the share price rises strongly:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Repurchase cost}\\uparrow$$</div>\n<p>therefore:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Account equity}\\downarrow$$</div>\n<p>Eventually:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{Margin call}}$$</div>\n<p>Again, the broker wants enough collateral protecting the position.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l2n-s44\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Long, margin and short positions compared</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Feature</th>\n<th class=\"ta-r\">Long cash purchase</th>\n<th class=\"ta-r\">Margin purchase</th>\n<th class=\"ta-r\">Short sale</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Expect price</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">Rise</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">Rise</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">Fall</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Borrow money?</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">No</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">Yes</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">Not necessarily money</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Borrow shares?</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">No</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">No</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">Yes</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Leverage</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">Usually no</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">Yes</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">Position involves collateral/margin</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Profit when price rises</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">Yes</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">Yes, magnified</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">No</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Profit when price falls</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">No</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">No, magnified loss</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">Yes</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Maximum price-related loss</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">Investment can fall toward 0</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">Can exceed initial equity depending on leverage/costs</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">Theoretically unlimited</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Financing/collateral concerns</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">Low</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">Important</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">Important</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>That comparison is much more useful than memorizing the three topics separately.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l2n-s45\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">The deeper principle behind margin and shorting</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Both involve <strong>obligations</strong>.</p>\n<p>With margin:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{You owe money}}$$</div>\n<p>With short selling:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{You owe shares}}$$</div>\n<p>That obligation does not disappear merely because the market moves against you.</p>\n<p>That's why these strategies have different risk characteristics from simply owning an asset outright.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l2n-s46\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Evaluate this statement: \"Margin increases return\"</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Incomplete.</p>\n<p>A good answer would be:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Margin increases the investor's exposure relative to the amount of equity committed. As a result, favourable price movements generate larger percentage gains on equity, but adverse movements also create larger percentage losses. Interest expense and margin-call risk further reduce the attractiveness of leverage.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>The chapter itself explicitly identifies both magnified gains and losses.</p>\n<p>The keyword is:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{magnifies}}$$</div>\n<p>not:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{improves}$$</div>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l2n-s47\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Evaluate: \"The secondary market is less important because firms don't raise money there\"</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Poor argument.</p>\n<p>Primary markets provide:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{capital raising}$$</div>\n<p>but secondary markets provide:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{liquidity}\n+\n\\text{price discovery}$$</div>\n<p>which the chapter explicitly identifies.</p>\n<p>Therefore, primary and secondary markets perform <strong>different but complementary functions</strong>.</p>\n<p>You cannot judge one as useless merely because it doesn't perform the other's function.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l2n-s48\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Evaluate: \"A dealer is basically another name for a broker\"</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Wrong.</p>\n<p>A broker primarily facilitates a transaction between parties.</p>\n<p>A dealer is itself a counterparty.</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{Broker matches}}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{Dealer trades}}$$</div>\n<p>That distinction affects inventory risk, liquidity provision and bid-ask quotations.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l2n-s49\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Evaluate: \"International diversification automatically lowers risk\"</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Too simplistic.</p>\n<p>International diversification can reduce risk when foreign investments do not move perfectly with domestic markets. The chapter specifically notes that foreign markets do not necessarily move together with the U.S. market.</p>\n<p>But international investing also introduces:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{currency risk}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{political/government risk}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{different regulatory environments}$$</div>\n<p>Therefore:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\n\\text{International diversification can reduce some risks while introducing others}\n}$$</div>\n<p>That's the analytical answer.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l2n-s50\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Evaluate: \"If a stock is liquid, it is safe\"</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>No.</p>\n<p>Liquidity answers:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Can I readily trade this security?</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Risk answers:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>How uncertain is my outcome?</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>A very volatile stock may still trade millions of shares per day.</p>\n<p>It can be:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{highly liquid}}$$</div>\n<p>and simultaneously:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{high risk}}$$</div>\n<p>This builds directly on Chapter 1.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l2n-s51\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">A full case to integrate the chapter</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Suppose TechCo wants RM500 million to finance new factories.</p>\n<p>It decides to sell shares publicly for the first time.</p>\n<p>An investment bank purchases the issue, creates a syndicate, assists with the prospectus and conducts presentations to investors.</p>\n<p>Shares are ultimately distributed to public investors.</p>\n<p>One month later, Investor A sells the stock to Investor B.</p>\n<p>Investor C buys TechCo shares using 50% margin.</p>\n<p>Investor D believes TechCo is severely overvalued and shorts the shares.</p>\n<p>Now classify everything.</p>\n<h4>TechCo's initial sale</h4>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{Primary market}}$$</div>\n<p>because new securities are being issued.</p>\n<p>Since shares are long-term ownership claims:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{Capital market}}$$</div>\n<hr />\n<h4>Investment bank</h4>\n<p>Acts as underwriter and distribution intermediary.</p>\n<p>It helps bear the risk of reselling the issue.</p>\n<hr />\n<h4>Investor A sells to Investor B later</h4>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{Secondary market}}$$</div>\n<p>TechCo receives none of this resale money.</p>\n<p>The transaction contributes to liquidity and market pricing.</p>\n<hr />\n<h4>Investor C</h4>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{Long + leverage}}$$</div>\n<p>They expect price appreciation but borrow part of the purchase cost.</p>\n<p>If TechCo rises:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{equity return amplified}$$</div>\n<p>If TechCo falls:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{loss amplified}$$</div>\n<p>Potential margin call.</p>\n<hr />\n<h4>Investor D</h4>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{Short}}$$</div>\n<p>They borrow shares, sell them now and hope to repurchase later at a lower price.</p>\n<p>If TechCo rises substantially:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{losses can become very large}$$</div>\n<p>That's basically the whole chapter operating inside one scenario.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l2n-s52\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">The six things I would master hardest for an exam</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Rather than treating all 41 slides equally, I'd concentrate on the relationships that generate questions.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Core idea</th>\n<th>What you must really understand</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Primary vs secondary</td>\n<td>Who receives the money and whether securities are new</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Money vs capital</td>\n<td>Short-term vs long-term nature</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Broker vs dealer</td>\n<td>Agent/matching vs principal/market maker</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Bid vs ask</td>\n<td>Dealer buys at bid and sells at ask</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Margin</td>\n<td>Debt causes percentage gains <strong>and losses</strong> on equity to be magnified</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Short selling</td>\n<td>Borrow → sell → buy back → return; limited upside and theoretically unlimited downside</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>If those six are strong, most definitions become easy.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l2n-s53\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Bloom level: Remember</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>You should be able to define accurately:</p>\n<p>primary market, secondary market, money market, capital market, IPO, underwriting, prospectus, broker, dealer, bid, ask, margin and short sale.</p>\n<p>But that is the lowest level.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l2n-s54\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Bloom level: Understand</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>You should be able to explain <strong>why</strong>:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{secondary markets create liquidity}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{dealers have bid and ask prices}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{underwriters exist}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{international investment creates FX risk}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{leverage magnifies outcomes}$$</div>\n<p>Not merely state that they do.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l2n-s55\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Bloom level: Apply</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Given:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>\"XYZ issues new shares to raise RM200 million.\"</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>You should identify:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{Primary capital market}}$$</div>\n<p>Given:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>\"A shareholder sells existing XYZ shares to another investor.\"</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>You should identify:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{Secondary capital market}}$$</div>\n<p>Given:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>\"A dealer quotes RM9.80-RM10.00.\"</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>If you sell:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{RM}9.80$$</div>\n<p>If you buy:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{RM}10.00$$</div>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l2n-s56\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Bloom level: Analyse</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Suppose a stock falls 20%.</p>\n<p>Investor A bought entirely with cash.</p>\n<p>Investor B used 50% margin.</p>\n<p>You should immediately reason:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{A's loss}\\approx20\\%$$</div>\n<p>while, ignoring financing costs:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{B's equity loss}\\approx40\\%$$</div>\n<p>Then explain <strong>why</strong>:</p>\n<p>because the debt remains while the decline is absorbed by a smaller equity base.</p>\n<p>That's analysis.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l2n-s57\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Bloom level: Evaluate</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Suppose someone says:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>\"I'll use maximum leverage because I'm highly confident the stock will rise.\"</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>A strong investment answer is not:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>\"That's risky.\"</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>It should evaluate the asymmetry:</p>\n<p>Confidence about expected direction does not eliminate uncertainty. Leverage increases sensitivity of equity to forecast errors, introduces financing cost and creates margin-call risk. A correct long-term thesis can still generate a forced loss if adverse short-term price movements exhaust the investor's margin capacity.</p>\n<p>That's much stronger reasoning.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l2n-s58\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Bloom level: Create</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Suppose you're asked:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Design a market-entry and trading strategy for an investor who wants exposure to a foreign company but is worried about FX movements, requires high liquidity and does not want leverage.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Now you need to combine:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{international market access}$$</div>\n<p>with:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{currency risk}$$</div>\n<p>with:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{secondary-market liquidity}$$</div>\n<p>while explicitly excluding:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{margin borrowing}$$</div>\n<p>That's no longer recall.</p>\n<p>You're designing an investment approach from the chapter's principles.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l2n-s59\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">The chapter compressed into one mental model</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>You can reduce Chapter 2 to this:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">  Companies need capital\n      │\n      ▼\n  Primary market creates/distributes securities\n      │\n      ▼\n  Secondary market lets investors trade them\n      │\n      ▼\n  Brokers/dealers/other systems execute transactions\n      │\n      ▼\n  Trading establishes liquidity and prices\n      │\n      ▼\n  Regulation protects market integrity\n      │\n      ▼\n  Investors choose long, leveraged or short exposures\n</code></pre>\n<p>That is Chapter 2 at the conceptual level.</p>\n<p>And the most important technical lesson is probably this:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\n\\text{Market direction}\n+\n\\text{position structure}\n=\n\\text{investor outcome}\n}$$</div>\n<p>A 20% movement in a stock does <strong>not</strong> imply every investor earns or loses 20%.</p>\n<p>A cash long investor, leveraged long investor and short seller can experience completely different outcomes from the exact same price movement.</p>\n<p>That is the conceptual jump Chapter 2 is trying to teach.</p></div></details></div></details>");
